<article class="env-definition" id="03JL">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03JL">Definition <span data-tag="03JL">68.3.1</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $X$ be an algebraic space over $S$, and let $U$ be a scheme over $S$. Let $f : U \to X$ be a morphism over $S$. We say the <em>fibres of $f$ are universally bounded</em><a class="footnotemark" href="#a0001370132" id="a0001370132-mark"><sup>1</sup></a> if there exists an integer $n$ such that for all fields $k$ and all morphisms $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(k) \to X$ the fibre product $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(k) \times _ X U$ is a finite scheme over $k$ whose degree over $k$ is $\leq n$. </p>
</article>